1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,154
At the fest, Aarav turns up
the amplifier for a song.

2
00:00:03,454 --> 00:00:05,889
The loud parts sound harsh and broken.

3
00:00:06,189 --> 00:00:08,922
On a scope, the tops of
the wave are sliced flat.

4
00:00:09,222 --> 00:00:12,009
The transistor is resting
in the wrong place.

5
00:00:12,009 --> 00:00:13,645
Let's find the right one.

6
00:00:14,145 --> 00:00:16,693
Think of a swing, hanging
between two walls.

7
00:00:17,043 --> 00:00:19,665
Hang it close to one wall, and push.

8
00:00:20,015 --> 00:00:21,528
It bangs into the wall.

9
00:00:21,528 --> 00:00:23,461
Part of each swing is cut off.

10
00:00:23,811 --> 00:00:25,557
Now hang it right in the middle.

11
00:00:25,907 --> 00:00:29,534
It swings fully both ways,
without touching either wall.

12
00:00:30,184 --> 00:00:32,617
A transistor amplifier is the same.

13
00:00:32,617 --> 00:00:34,792
It needs a good resting place.

14
00:00:35,442 --> 00:00:38,627
Here is the common emitter
circuit on the breadboard.

15
00:00:38,977 --> 00:00:42,756
A small supply, VBB,
drives the base through RB.

16
00:00:43,106 --> 00:00:47,010
A bigger supply, VCC, feeds
the collector through RC.

17
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,692
With no signal, steady currents flow.

18
00:00:49,692 --> 00:00:51,432
This is the DC condition.

19
00:00:51,782 --> 00:00:53,082
Turn RB down.

20
00:00:53,082 --> 00:00:54,874
The base current rises.

21
00:00:55,224 --> 00:00:58,466
The collector current
rises too, beta times more.

22
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:00,146
Here beta is one fifty.

23
00:01:00,496 --> 00:01:04,799
And VCE falls, because more
voltage drops across RC.

24
00:01:05,449 --> 00:01:08,437
Let's turn this into
a graph, step by step.

25
00:01:08,787 --> 00:01:09,841
Base loop.

26
00:01:09,841 --> 00:01:13,136
VBB equals IBRB, plus VBE.

27
00:01:13,836 --> 00:01:18,201
So IB equals VBB minus VBE, over RB.

28
00:01:18,901 --> 00:01:21,479
Then IC equals beta times IB.

29
00:01:22,179 --> 00:01:23,543
Collector loop.

30
00:01:23,543 --> 00:01:26,856
VCC equals ICRC, plus VCE.

31
00:01:27,556 --> 00:01:30,719
So VCE equals VCC minus ICRC.

32
00:01:30,719 --> 00:01:32,675
That is a straight line.

33
00:01:33,375 --> 00:01:34,893
Set IC to zero.

34
00:01:34,893 --> 00:01:37,750
VCE equals VCC, twelve volts.

35
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:39,314
The cut off point.

36
00:01:40,014 --> 00:01:41,553
Set VCE to zero.

37
00:01:41,553 --> 00:01:44,620
IC is VCC over RC, six milliamperes.

38
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,128
The saturation point.

39
00:01:46,828 --> 00:01:48,226
Join the two points.

40
00:01:48,226 --> 00:01:50,843
This straight line is the DC load line.

41
00:01:51,493 --> 00:01:54,633
Every resting state of this
circuit sits on this line.

42
00:01:54,983 --> 00:01:59,071
Where it actually rests is the
operating point, or Q point.

43
00:01:59,421 --> 00:02:01,040
Q stands for quiescent.

44
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:03,272
It means quiet, with no signal.

45
00:02:03,622 --> 00:02:05,900
Now add a small signal at the base.

46
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:08,076
The Q point swings along the line.

47
00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:11,690
With Q in the middle,
the output wave is clean.

48
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:13,998
Move Q close to saturation.

49
00:02:13,998 --> 00:02:15,750
The swing hits the end.

50
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,986
The bottom of the output
wave is sliced off.

51
00:02:18,986 --> 00:02:20,222
That is clipping.

52
00:02:20,572 --> 00:02:22,294
Move Q close to cut off.

53
00:02:22,294 --> 00:02:23,958
Now the top gets sliced.

54
00:02:24,308 --> 00:02:28,624
So for the biggest clean swing, keep
Q near the middle of the load line.

55
00:02:29,274 --> 00:02:31,041
The load line is the road.

56
00:02:31,041 --> 00:02:33,047
The Q point is where you park.

57
00:02:33,847 --> 00:02:34,966
Worked example.

58
00:02:34,966 --> 00:02:36,304
Find the Q point.

59
00:02:36,654 --> 00:02:38,723
VCC is twelve volts.

60
00:02:38,723 --> 00:02:40,732
RC is two kilo ohms.

61
00:02:40,732 --> 00:02:42,414
Beta is one fifty.

62
00:02:42,764 --> 00:02:44,715
VBB is three volts.

63
00:02:44,715 --> 00:02:47,747
RB is one hundred fifteen kilo ohms.

64
00:02:48,097 --> 00:02:53,223
IB is three minus zero point seven,
over one hundred fifteen kilo ohms.

65
00:02:53,573 --> 00:02:54,864
That is twenty microamperes.

66
00:02:55,564 --> 00:02:57,794
IC is one fifty times twenty microamperes.

67
00:02:57,794 --> 00:02:58,817
Three milliamperes.

68
00:02:59,517 --> 00:03:03,631
VCE is twelve, minus three
milliamperes times two kilo ohms.

69
00:03:03,981 --> 00:03:05,365
That is six volts.

70
00:03:06,065 --> 00:03:08,533
So Q is at six volts
and three milliamperes.

71
00:03:08,533 --> 00:03:10,662
Right in the middle of the load line.

72
00:03:11,312 --> 00:03:12,776
Now, the exam trap.

73
00:03:13,126 --> 00:03:16,780
Many students think a Q point near
saturation gives a bigger output.

74
00:03:17,130 --> 00:03:18,030
It does not.

75
00:03:18,013 --> 00:03:19,024
The wave clips.

76
00:03:19,024 --> 00:03:21,356
The middle gives the biggest clean swing.

77
00:03:22,006 --> 00:03:24,880
Also, the load line ends use RC only.

78
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:26,487
RB is not in them.

79
00:03:26,837 --> 00:03:28,458
Where is this asked?

80
00:03:28,458 --> 00:03:30,319
In PUC board papers.

81
00:03:30,969 --> 00:03:36,201
Draw the load line, mark the Q
point, and find IB, IC and VCE.

82
00:03:36,851 --> 00:03:37,805
Try this.

83
00:03:37,805 --> 00:03:43,855
VCC is ten volts, RC is one kilo
ohm, VBB is four point seven volts,

84
00:03:43,855 --> 00:03:48,242
RB is one hundred kilo ohms,
and beta is one hundred.

85
00:03:48,242 --> 00:03:50,103
Where is the Q point?

86
00:03:55,503 --> 00:03:56,403
C.

87
00:03:55,797 --> 00:03:57,334
Six volts and four milliamperes.

88
00:03:57,684 --> 00:04:00,844
IB is four volts over
one hundred kilo ohms.

89
00:04:00,844 --> 00:04:02,190
Forty microamperes.

90
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:04,883
IC is one hundred times
forty microamperes.

91
00:04:04,883 --> 00:04:05,898
Four milliamperes.

92
00:04:06,248 --> 00:04:10,404
VCE is ten, minus four
milliamperes times one kilo ohm.

93
00:04:10,404 --> 00:04:11,326
Six volts.

94
00:04:11,976 --> 00:04:13,523
Screenshot this card.

95
00:04:16,723 --> 00:04:18,678
Next, voltage divider bias.

96
00:04:18,678 --> 00:04:21,852
How do we stop the Q
point drifting with heat?
